{
  "name": "Quatre Bras",
  "campaign": "hundred-days-1815",
  "summary": "16 June 1815, and the case the Italian campaign could never make: open rye, gentle ground, and horse looking for infantry that has not finished forming square. Ney has to take a crossroads that is the only lateral road between Wellington and Blücher, and he starts the afternoon with more men on the ground than Wellington has. He does not finish it that way. Both armies walk on up the same two roads all day — Picton, the Brunswickers and the Guards from the north, Jérôme and Kellermann's cuirassiers from the south — so the battle is not about a position but about who is still arriving. The Field is a reading of the ground between the crossroads and Frasnes rather than a survey of it: the Bois de Bossu down the west of the chaussée, Gémioncourt in the middle of the open, and the pond and the hamlet holding the east.",
